@page "/projects/recordshop"

<PageTitle>RecordShop Project | Nazmul Hussain</PageTitle>

<div class="project-back">
    <NavLink class="project-back-link" href="/projects">
        &larr; Back to projects
    </NavLink>
</div>

<header class="project-detail-hero">
    <p class="project-type">Solo project</p>
    <h1>RecordShop</h1>

    <p class="project-introduction">
        A full-stack record catalogue for browsing, searching and managing albums,
        built with a layered ASP.NET Core Web API, TDD, SQL Server and a Blazor frontend.
    </p>

    <ul class="project-technologies" aria-label="Technologies used">
        <li>C#</li>
        <li>.NET 8</li>
        <li>ASP.NET Core Web API</li>
        <li>Blazor</li>
        <li>Entity Framework Core</li>
        <li>SQL Server</li>
        <li>NUnit</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="project-actions">
        <a href="https://github.com/Nazmul5765/Record-Shop"
           target="_blank"
           rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Backend repository
        </a>

        <a href="https://github.com/Nazmul5765/record-shop-frontend"
           target="_blank"
           rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Frontend repository
        </a>
    </div>
</header>

<section class="project-overview" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
    <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-section-label">Overview</p>
        <h2 id="overview-title">A full-stack catalogue for managing records</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="project-section-content">
        <p>
            I developed RecordShop as my solo project during the Northcoders
            Enterprise Engineering Bootcamp. My goal was to apply the backend,
            database, testing and frontend skills I had learned by building a
            complete full-stack application with a strong focus on backend development.
        </p>

        <p>
            Users can browse, search, add, update and delete albums through the
            Blazor frontend. The frontend sends HTTP requests to an ASP.NET Core
            Web API, which uses Entity Framework Core to manage data stored in
            SQL Server.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="project-decisions" aria-labelledby="decisions-title">
    <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-section-label">Engineering</p>
        <h2 id="decisions-title">From requirements to implementation</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="decisions-grid">
        <article class="decision-card">
            <p class="decision-label">Challenge</p>
            <h3>Managing responsibilities across the application</h3>
            <p>
                The application needed to handle HTTP requests, business logic and
                database access without placing all of these responsibilities in
                one class.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="decision-card">
            <p class="decision-label">Approach</p>
            <h3>Using a layered backend architecture</h3>
            <p>
                I separated the backend into controller, service and repository
                layers, using dependency injection to connect them. The Blazor
                frontend communicates with the API through HTTP requests.
            </p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="project-features" aria-labelledby="features-title">
    <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-section-label">Features</p>
        <h2 id="features-title">Core application capabilities</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="features-list">
        <li>
            <span class="feature-number">01</span>
            <div>
                <h3>Browse and view albums</h3>
                <p>
                    Browse the complete album catalogue and open an individual album
                    to view its details.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>

        <li>
            <span class="feature-number">02</span>
            <div>
                <h3>Search for albums</h3>
                <p>
                    Find albums by their ID, title or artist using search options
                    provided through the frontend.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>

        <li>
            <span class="feature-number">03</span>
            <div>
                <h3>Manage the album catalogue</h3>
                <p>
                    Add new albums, edit existing album details and remove albums
                    that are no longer required.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>

        <li>
            <span class="feature-number">04</span>
            <div>
            <h3>Persistence and health monitoring</h3>
            <p>
                    Store album data with Entity Framework Core and SQL Server, with
                    health checks for both the API and its database connection.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="project-testing" aria-labelledby="testing-title">
    <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-section-label">Quality</p>
        <h2 id="testing-title">Developing backend behaviour with TDD</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="testing-content">
        <p>
            I used test-driven development for much of the backend, writing tests
            before implementing the related functionality. NUnit tests cover the
            controller, service and repository layers, including CRUD operations,
            search behaviour and HTTP responses. Moq isolates dependencies, while
            repository tests use an in-memory database and Shouldly keeps assertions readable.
        </p>

        <ul class="testing-tools" aria-label="Testing tools used">
            <li>NUnit</li>
            <li>Moq</li>
            <li>Shouldly</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="project-reflection" aria-labelledby="reflection-title">
    <div class="reflection-content">
        <p class="project-section-label">Reflection</p>
        <h2 id="reflection-title">What I developed through this project</h2>

        <p>
            I better understood how a request moves from the Blazor frontend through
            the controller, service and repository layers to the database. Testing
            each backend layer also helped me learn how to isolate dependencies,
            verify behaviour and monitor whether the API and database are healthy.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="project-navigation">
        <NavLink class="project-navigation-link" href="/projects/calico">
            Next project: Calico &rarr;
        </NavLink>
    </div>
</section>
